# THIS FILE IS MANAGED BY CHEZMOI!
#
# sshd_config for the *user-session* OpenSSH server -- an sshd running inside
# the interactive desktop session under a Task Scheduler at-logon task, not the
# stock SYSTEM service. Registered by
# .chezmoiscripts/00-nt/run_onchange_after_035-sshd-user-session.ps1.tmpl,
# launched by ~/.local/bin/sshd-user-session.ps1. Knobs live in
# .chezmoidata/sshd.toml.
#
# Why it exists: the stock service runs as NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM in session 0 and
# mints a brand new token per connection (S4U for public-key auth), so every
# SSH child gets a freshly allocated logon session -- which means an empty
# drive-letter view and, being session 0, no shell namespace providers. Mapped
# network drives are simply invisible over scp. Win32-OpenSSH short-circuits
# that whole path when sshd is not SYSTEM and the authenticating user's SID
# matches the process SID: it hands the child sshd's own process token instead.
# Run sshd from the desktop session and the mapped drives are there.
#
# Forward slashes throughout. Win32-OpenSSH accepts them and they avoid having
# to think about backslash escaping in the config parser. .chezmoi.homeDir is
# already forward-slashed on Windows, so it interpolates directly -- and it is
# derived per-machine rather than hardcoded, because the account name and the
# home directory basename are not the same thing here.

Port {{ .sshd.port }}

# Only loopback is static. The {{ .sshd.interface }} interface's addresses are
# appended at launch by the wrapper as `-o ListenAddress=<addr>` arguments,
# because OpenSSH has no interface-name form of ListenAddress and a hardcoded
# 100.x/fd7a: address would rot if the node were re-registered on the tailnet.
#
# Two things make that split work, both verified against sshd -T on this build:
#
#   1. ListenAddress entries from -o and from this file *accumulate*; they do
#      not override each other.
#   2. A ListenAddress with no port attached is resolved against the configured
#      Port at the end of parsing, not at the point it is read. So the wrapper
#      passes bare addresses and they land on the Port set above.
#
# The corollary is that Port must appear only here: `-o Port=` accumulates too,
# so passing it on the command line would bind every address on *both* ports.
#
# Keeping loopback in the file rather than in the wrapper is deliberate. sshd
# exits only when it cannot bind *any* listener, so a static loopback entry
# guarantees the daemon still comes up -- reachable at least locally, and
# visible in the log -- on a boot where Tailscale has not come up yet.
ListenAddress 127.0.0.1
ListenAddress ::1

HostKey {{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/sshd/ssh_host_ed25519_key
AuthorizedKeysFile {{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.ssh/authorized_keys
PidFile {{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/sshd/sshd.pid

# Public key only. This port is reachable from the tailnet, and a password
# prompt here would be an online guessing oracle against the Windows account
# password. ~/.ssh/authorized_keys is already managed by this repository
# (dot_ssh/private_authorized_keys), so there is a key to let in.
#
# KbdInteractive is disabled explicitly rather than left at its default:
# Win32-OpenSSH will otherwise happily fall back to it and prompt for the same
# password that PasswordAuthentication just refused.
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no

# Deliberately NOT carried over from the stock C:\ProgramData\ssh\sshd_config:
#
#     Match Group administrators
#             AuthorizedKeysFile __PROGRAMDATA__/ssh/administrators_authorized_keys
#
# It redirects key lookup away from ~/.ssh/authorized_keys for any account in
# the Administrators group, which this one is, and so would defeat the
# AuthorizedKeysFile set above entirely.

Subsystem sftp sftp-server.exe
